WEBVTT

hook-1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.714 line:78% position:50% align:center
A wood frog can survive a freeze that stops its

hook-2
00:00:03.714 --> 00:00:05.180 line:78% position:50% align:center
heartbeat—then thaw and recover.

freeze-1
00:00:05.180 --> 00:00:08.032 line:78% position:50% align:center
Much of its body water can turn to ice,

freeze-2
00:00:08.032 --> 00:00:09.300 line:78% position:50% align:center
mainly outside its cells.

location-1
00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:11.068 line:78% position:50% align:center
As extracellular ice grows,

location-2
00:00:11.068 --> 00:00:14.605 line:78% position:50% align:center
water leaves the cells. That dehydration helps keep

location-3
00:00:14.605 --> 00:00:17.700 line:78% position:50% align:center
dangerous ice crystals from forming inside them.

chemistry-1
00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:19.700 line:78% position:50% align:center
Its liver rapidly releases glucose,

chemistry-2
00:00:19.700 --> 00:00:22.900 line:78% position:50% align:center
while urea has already built up before freezing.

protection-1
00:00:22.900 --> 00:00:25.915 line:78% position:50% align:center
These cryoprotectants limit ice formation and help

protection-2
00:00:25.915 --> 00:00:28.500 line:78% position:50% align:center
protect cells through freezing and thawing.

payoff-1
00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:32.462 line:78% position:50% align:center
As the frog thaws, its heart starts beating first;

payoff-2
00:00:32.462 --> 00:00:34.663 line:78% position:50% align:center
breathing and leg reflexes follow.

payoff-3
00:00:34.663 --> 00:00:38.625 line:78% position:50% align:center
It survived by controlling the freeze—not by staying unfrozen.
